<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Every <loc> here is the canonical URL of a page, and it is the same URL
  that page puts in its own <link rel="canonical"> and that every internal
  link on the site points at. A sitemap that disagrees with the canonicals
  is worse than no sitemap: it hands a search engine two addresses for one
  page and lets them compete.

  /care-pass is a public product page, so it IS listed: the return from
  the provider carries a reference in the query string, and the page
  declares this canonical so the two addresses cannot compete.

  Only fetchable, indexable pages belong in this file. Not listed, on
  purpose: /admin.html, /complete-listing.html and /my-care.html (private,
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex), /404.html (noindex), and /listing — every listing
  now has a real address at /rooms/<slug> and those are in the SECOND
  sitemap, https://arzuz.com/sitemap-listings.xml, which is generated from
  the database because listings come and go. /listing?id=<uuid> still
  answers, but it declares /rooms/<slug> as its canonical, so it is not
  listed here and does not compete with the room's own page.

  The lastmod field is the only one Google actually reads here, and only while it
  stays verifiably accurate. <changefreq> and <priority> have been ignored
  by Google for years and are kept for other engines. A fabricated lastmod
  is worse than none, so this date changes only when the page's own content
  changes — not on every deploy.

  Plain UTF-8, no byte-order mark: a BOM makes Search Console reject the
  file outright.
-->
